      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The purpose of this randomized, double-blind placebo-controlled study was to evaluate the effect of nefopam, a centrally acting antinociceptive compound, on the development of hyperalgesia after sternotomy. Preventive strategy giving nefopam from the early stage of anesthesia was compared with a postoperative strategy only and placebo."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "This study was double-blinded and randomized."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "It was conducted in a single university hospital."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Ninety American Society of Anesthesiologists II to III patients scheduled for elective cardiac surgery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "Patients were assigned randomly to receive a 0.3-mg/kg bolus of nefopam at the induction of anesthesia followed by a continuous infusion of 0.065 mg/kg/h for 48 hours (G1), a 0.3-mg/kg bolus of nefopam at the end of surgery followed by a continuous infusion of 0.065 mg/kg/h for 48 hours (G2), or a placebo (G3). Postoperative analgesia was based on morphine patient-controlled analgesia and rescue analgesia when necessary. Postoperative hyperalgesia, pain scores, morphine consumption, and postoperative cognitive dysfunction were assessed for the first 48 hours and thereafter on postoperative days 4 and 7."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
            "content": "The postoperative extent of dynamic hyperalgesia and the decrease of the nociceptive threshold evaluated by von Frey filaments at the sternal midline were smaller in group 1 and group 2 compared with the placebo group at the 24th hour. The primary objective was the extent of hyperalgesia at the midline given as the mean (standard deviation [SD]) (4.4 [2.5] cm for G1, 4.1 [2.7] for G2, and 6.1 [2.7] cm for G3. The punctuate is given as mean (SD) (64 [43] g for G1, 68 [40.8] g for G2, and 32 [27] g for G3; with p < 0.05 for the comparisons of extent and punctuate hyperalgesia between G1 and G3 and G2 and G3). The extent of hyperalgesia was not significantly different among the 3 groups on days 2, 4, and 7 after surgery. There were no significant differences in pain scores, morphine consumption, or postoperative cognitive dysfunctions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Nefopam administered during the perioperative period slightly reduced acute hyperalgesia after cardiac surgery, but this was not associated with improved analgesic efficacy."
          }
        ]
      },
